-- title:
--   Primitive series modulo p-th powers for a formal group law
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field of characteristic $p$ for a prime $p$, let $n$ be a natural number, and let $F$ be an $n$-dimensional formal group law over $k$ in the project's sense: an $n$-tuple $F = (F_1,\dots,F_n)$ of power series in the variables indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,n \sqcup \mathrm{Fin}\,n$, each with zero constant term, with the linear coefficient of $F_i$ at the $j$-th variable of either copy equal to $1$ if $i = j$ and $0$ otherwise, and satisfying the associativity identity $F(F(X,Y),Z) = F(X,F(Y,Z))$ expressed by substitution into three groups of variables. Let $g$ be a multivariate power series in the variables indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,n$ over $k$ whose constant coefficient vanishes and whose coefficient at every exponent $\mathrm{single}\,m\,1$ vanishes, i.e. $g$ has no constant and no linear term. Assume its additive coboundary $\partial g = g(F(X,Y)) - g(X) - g(Y)$, formed by substituting $F$, respectively the first and the second copy of the variables, into $g$, lies in the ideal of $k[[X,Y]]$ generated by $\{X_s^p\}$ as $s$ ranges over $\mathrm{Fin}\,n \sqcup \mathrm{Fin}\,n$. Then $g$ lies in the ideal of $k[[X]]$ generated by $X_i^p$, $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,n$.
--
--   This is the statement that a power series without constant or linear term which is primitive modulo $p$-th powers — that is, which defines a primitive element on the Frobenius kernel of $F$ — already lies in the ideal of $p$-th powers of the variables. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld material on special formal $O_D$-modules, in the analysis of coboundaries invariant under substitution by a uniformiser.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvFormalGroup_mem_span_X_pow_of_addCoboundary_mem_span_of_coeff_single_eq_zero.lean

